  {
    "pkattachmentId": 76375,
    "bookid": 72,
    "chapterId": 131,
    "fileId": 49936,
    "type": "Figure",
    "diagnosis": "Extranodal marginal zone lymphoma (EMZL)",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "The proposed model of molecular pathogenesis of EMZL. The development of EMZL is the result of oncogenic cooperation among dysregulated immune responses and somatic genetic changes. The genetic changes vary considerably among EMZLs of various sites but commonly affect the molecular pathways important for marginal B&nbsp;cells, particularly those leading to NF-&kappa;B activation. In general, the canonical NF-&kappa;B pathway is activated by chronic B-cell receptor signalling triggered by autoantigens and genetic changes such as IGH::<em>BCL10</em>, IGH::<em>MALT1</em>, <em>BIRC3</em>::<em>MALT1</em>, and <em>TNFAIP3 </em>mutation/deletion, whereas the non-canonical NF-&kappa;B pathway is activated by helper T&nbsp;cells, soluble ligands (CD40, BAFF), and <em>BIRC3</em>::<em>MALT1</em> fusion. In gastric EMZL, helper T&nbsp;cells are triggered by <em>Helicobacter pylori</em>&ndash;mediated immune responses, whereas in thyroid EMZL, these may result indirectly from genetic inactivation of <em>CD274</em> (<em>PDL1</em>) and <em>TNFRSF14</em>. In salivary gland EMZL, <em>GPR34</em> activation is triggered by mutation, rarely translocation, but more commonly by ligands produced by lymphoepithelial lesions. Similarly, <em>CCR6</em> is activated by mutation and ligands from the inflamed epithelium in lesions of the salivary glands and stomach. Negative regulators are in black; positive regulators are in different colours.",
    "source": "Ming-Qinq Du copy from book:Haematolymphoid Tumours bookid:63 Original id:#30888 ",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "",
    "imageUrl": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/72/131/30888.jpg",
    "thumbnail": "https://tumourclassification.iarc.who.int/static/dzi/72/131/thumb_30888.jpg"
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 76376,
    "bookid": 72,
    "chapterId": 131,
    "fileId": 49937,
    "type": "Figure",
    "diagnosis": "Extranodal marginal zone lymphoma, gastric localization",
    "title": "",
    "legend": "<strong>A</strong>&nbsp;The lamina propria is replaced by dense sheets of lymphoma cells. <strong>B</strong>&nbsp;Extranodal marginal zone lymphoma lymphoepithelial lesion. The glands are infiltrated, partially destroyed, and focally expanded by the lymphoma cells. <strong>C,D</strong>&nbsp;The infiltrate is positive for CD20&nbsp;(<strong>C</strong>), with only scarce CD3-positive T&nbsp;cells&nbsp;(<strong>D</strong>). <strong>E</strong>&nbsp;A signet ring&ndash;like appearance of the residual glandular cells may be seen.<strong> E</strong>&nbsp;A signet ring&ndash;like appearance of the residual glandular cells may be seen.",
    "source": "Wah Cheuk copy from book:Haematolymphoid Tumours bookid:63 Original id:#31115 ",
